.docname {Introduction}
.include {docs}

vCortex turns a quick walk with a robot into a simple, measured model of a building's insides:
its walls, its rooms and the things inside them.

![From a walk to a digital twin](images/walk-to-twin.svg "From a walk to a digital twin, in three steps.")

## The problem

Many jobs need a model of the inside of a building: planning a renovation, simulating how
people move, or telling a robot where it can go. Drawings are often old or missing, and making
new ones by hand takes hours with a tape measure.

A robot with a **lidar** (a laser that measures distances all around it) can measure a room in
a few minutes. What it gives back is a **point cloud**: a huge set of dots, each one a spot the
laser touched. One room of 8.5 × 6.6 m gave **192,149 dots**.

The dots alone are not a model: nothing in them says "this is a wall" or "this is a desk".
Turning them into one is harder than it looks; [Challenges](challenges.qd) explains why.

## What vCortex is

vCortex is an app that reads a point cloud and builds a **digital twin** from it: a light
description of the building with walls, rooms and objects, all measured in metres.

- **Live.** While the robot walks, the map streams in and the twin is rebuilt every 3 seconds.
- **Offline.** It can also open a map saved earlier.
- **Side by side.** The page shows the raw dots on the left and the twin on the right.

![vCortex showing a real room](images/app-3d.png "A real room: the raw dots on the left, the digital twin on the right.")

## What vCortex solves

- **From dots to things.** It tells walls from furniture by their shape and position, not only
  by their height.
- **Clean input.** It drops stray dots that lie far from everything else.
- **Square and level.** It finds the floor, measures heights from it, and turns the map so the
  walls run straight along the axes.
- **Any source.** vCortex is not tied to one robot. Anything that can send the map can feed it.
- **Useful output.** It saves the twin in formats other tools read:

| Format | What it is | Good for |
|---|---|---|
| Twin (JSON) | Walls, rooms and objects, with names from IFC, the open standard for building data | Other apps and simulations |
| Floor plan (DXF) | A 2D drawing | CAD programs |
| Cleaned cloud (PLY) | The dots, without the noise | 3D tools |

![The same room from above](images/app-top.png "The same room from above. The twin is rotated to match the dots.")

## What to expect

On the room in the pictures, vCortex gave this result:

| Measure | Value |
|---|---|
| Dots kept after cleaning | 186,427 of 192,149 (97%) |
| Walls | 4 |
| Rooms | 1, of 8.52 × 6.55 m |
| Objects | 19 |
| Time for one model run | about 1.5 s |

It works well on rooms with straight walls that meet at right angles. Its limits today are listed
at the end of [Challenges](challenges.qd).
